#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175765 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175900 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cda8b343-e71d-5ec2-ae82-3f66dba09264" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Given the Graph of a Derivative"}] :question/id #uuid "403f94fd-793b-55eb-a7c4-14f97cf4ca79" :question/math-academy-id "e-338" :question/problem "The graph $y=g'(x),$ the derivative of $y=g(x),$ is shown below. Which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\nI. $g(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=1.5$\n\nII. $g(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=5$\n\nIII. $g(x)$ has an inflection point at $x=3.5$\n\n![](images/58/582d792bf3854b0060e23e171fdc0c72e6486d09b0f1be47552ec4338d3f8ba1.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $y=g'(x)$ intersects the $x$-axis at the points $A$ and $B,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/fb/fbfcc5d729dea273d9b60c2ef80e10696aa0631698a7cb4ff009f54fb59b1ea3.png)\n\nAn extremum of $y=g(x)$ occurs when the graph $y=g'(x)$ changes its sign. Checking the first derivative at the points $A$ and $B,$ we see that\n\n- the point $A,$ at $x=1.5,$ is a relative maximum of $g(x)$ since the derivative $g'(x)$ switches from positive to negative, and\n\n- the point $B,$ at $x=5,$ is a relative minimum of $g(x)$ since the derivative $g'(x)$ switches from negative to positive.\n\nTherefore, statements I and II are true.\n\nNow, an inflection point may occur where the second derivative $y=g''(x)$ is zero (the tangent line to $y=g'(x)$ is horizontal) or $y=g''(x)$ does not exist (a rapid change in the slope). Indeed, at the point $x=3.5,$ which we label as $C,$ we see that the slope of $g'(x)$ is zero, meaning that $g''(x)=0.$\n\n![](images/e4/e4e945f8e45cd67982246dbd1798f3958eff114f7dc04ff98c3a30f5f570eff9.png)\n\nWe obtain an inflection point if the slope of the tangent line to $y=g'(x)$ changes its sign. Thus, we have an inflection point at $C$, where the slope goes from negative to positive. Therefore, statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, all three statements are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175796 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175947 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f73c50f7-fa8c-5d23-8699-e7e074f4251b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Inflection Points and Intervals of Concavity Given the Graph of a Derivative"}] :question/id #uuid "7a25d851-2f79-5e70-bde2-eda99697fe3e" :question/math-academy-id "e-6851" :question/problem "The graph of $y=f'(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is defined on the interval $[-2,3]$ as shown below. Find all of the inflection points of $y=f(x)$ for $x\\in(-2,3).$ \n\n![](images/1d/1da07563588809f268054c88f684f47c701f4daeeb2bfdcb5c01d3f4d9aaa397.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, note that $f'(x)$ exists at every point on the interval $x\\in [-2,3].$\n\nWe need to investigate the behavior of $f''(x).$ We recall the following:\n\n- When $f'(x)$ is increasing, we have $f''(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f'(x)$ is decreasing, we have $f''(x)<       0.$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ has an inflection point at $x=a,$ if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n- $f''(x)=0$ or $f''(x)$ is undefined, and\n\n- $f''(x)$ changes its sign (from negative to positive or vice versa) at that point.\n\nWe summarize the information from the given graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-2,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,3)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f'$ | $\\searrow$ |  | $\\searrow$ |  | $\\nearrow$ |\n| $f''$ | $-$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $-$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $+$ |\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f''(x)$ changes its sign at the point $x=1.$ Therefore, $x=1$ is a point of inflection of $y=f(x).$\n\nNote that the sign of $f''(x)$ does not change at $x=-1,$ and so this is *not* a point of inflection."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516857 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175947 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f73c50f7-fa8c-5d23-8699-e7e074f4251b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Inflection Points and Intervals of Concavity Given the Graph of a Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "302fde07-bc48-5c72-b8b3-034b319ccbbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516212} {:answer/id #uuid "524b76af-8aa4-5c07-b317-b7edfa6e3f76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$  and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516282} {:answer/id #uuid "a7760ce3-fd8a-5b32-94d8-4cb2bb52143a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2,$ $x=-1$  and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516460} {:answer/id #uuid "d246892f-bf9b-50d6-a3de-71fe4d2e1a03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516557} {:answer/id #uuid "e1ffc3b9-671e-5a5a-8aa7-e17a9cbf4f00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7760ce3-fd8a-5b32-94d8-4cb2bb52143a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2,$ $x=-1$  and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516460} :answer-field/id #uuid "18eb4c75-46a0-5a83-b09d-9588dd282967" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516660}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d4806679-d98f-5bc2-87be-0a6d9963a840" :question/math-academy-id "q-104009" :question/problem "![](images/a1/a16176b3fe7ff15ca90aef4a829d53d5f8e64c16c9e0f623ca0d7378cc7fa8f1.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is defined on the interval $[-3,3]$ as shown above. Find all of the inflection points of $y=f(x)$ for $x\\in (-3,3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists at every point on the interval $x\\in [-3,3].$ \n\nWe need to investigate the behavior of ${f}^{″}(x).$ We recall the following:\n\n- When ${f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{″}(x)>0.$\n\n- When ${f}^{′}(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ has an inflection point at $x=a,$ if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n- ${f}^{″}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{″}(x)$ is undefined, and\n\n- ${f}^{″}(x)$ changes its sign (from negative to positive or vice versa) at that point.\n\nWe summarize the information from the given graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-3,-2)$ | $-2$ | $(-2,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,3)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $↗$ |  | $↘$ |  | $↗$ |  | $↘$ |\n| ${f}^{″}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $+$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $-$ |\n\nFrom the table, we conclude that ${f}^{″}(x)$ changes its sign at the points $x=-2,$  $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ Therefore, $x=-2,$ $x=-1$ and $x=1$ are  the points of inflection of $y=f(x).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516837 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175947 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f73c50f7-fa8c-5d23-8699-e7e074f4251b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Inflection Points and Intervals of Concavity Given the Graph of a Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d0f4454-3e2f-5733-8bb0-b8e1a8a2990e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516166} {:answer/id #uuid "48cd4f95-5f94-5695-82e8-0b0840d549a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516259} {:answer/id #uuid "75334e32-7f03-5dfa-934f-b50d3ecfae98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516373} {:answer/id #uuid "a2b4b19f-126b-5d73-9cfc-c884d8dca55b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516447} {:answer/id #uuid "bc9cc757-6fcd-59be-b35b-eae8323497d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75334e32-7f03-5dfa-934f-b50d3ecfae98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516373} :answer-field/id #uuid "6eb636e9-e746-565d-8b30-702d3ffe597c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516700}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b39bf78a-2c45-5cec-8ca0-7f6b069d4c47" :question/math-academy-id "q-104018" :question/problem "![](images/e9/e9da73fadd389e8cb47c58eb980e0f2dee6b36f3bb0475466090bd5ac3bfb35c.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is defined on the interval $[-4,4]$ and shown above. Find all the inflection points for $y=f(x)$ on $(-4,4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists at every point of the interval $x\\in [-4,4].$\n\nWe need to investigate the behavior of ${f}^{″}(x).$ We recall the following:\n\n- When ${f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{″}(x)>0.$\n\n- When ${f}^{′}(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ has an inflection point at $x=a,$ if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n- ${f}^{″}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{″}(x)$ is undefined, and\n\n- ${f}^{″}(x)$ changes its sign (from negative to positive or vice versa) at that point.\n\nWe summarize the information from the given graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-4,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,4)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $↗$ |  | $\\text{const.}$ |  | $↗$ | $0$ | $↘$ |\n| ${f}^{″}$ | $+$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $0$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n\nFrom the table, we conclude that ${f}^{″}(x)$ changes its sign only  at the point $x=2.$ Therefore, $x=2$ is the only point of inflection of $y=f(x).$\n\n**Watch out!** The second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ does not change the sign when it passes through $x=-1$ or $x=1.$  Hence, neither $x=-1$ nor $x=1$ are points of inflection."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516833 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175947 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f73c50f7-fa8c-5d23-8699-e7e074f4251b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Inflection Points and Intervals of Concavity Given the Graph of a Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20f2b55d-569c-51eb-b9b0-8f386f1d3fd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516175} {:answer/id #uuid "40e0d2de-b084-5a07-afc2-79680730e646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516240} {:answer/id #uuid "5dfd7198-ef12-5126-93c7-69d69036ae74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516313} {:answer/id #uuid "6b97aa84-fc94-58d4-91a9-22371fae28a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516347} {:answer/id #uuid "aaa76cea-fc52-5a9f-a2a3-c9175a2ee88b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5dfd7198-ef12-5126-93c7-69d69036ae74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516313} :answer-field/id #uuid "6413a700-8297-5d14-a631-3c517a8e3a82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516694}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aa64cd03-641d-5fbe-b490-fd0f93407035" :question/math-academy-id "q-104027" :question/problem "![](images/9f/9ffebb1757b03211d1dd7d8d53ea8a02bd5262534fb868b44662f5a4e5f6fbd8.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is defined on the interval $[-3,3]$ as shown above. Find all of the inflection points of $y=f(x)$ for $x\\in (-3,3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists at every point on the interval $x\\in [-3,3].$\n\nWe need to investigate the behavior of ${f}^{″}(x).$ We recall the following:\n\n- When ${f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{″}(x)>0.$\n\n- When ${f}^{′}(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ has an inflection point at $x=a,$ if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n- ${f}^{″}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{″}(x)$ is undefined, and\n\n- ${f}^{″}(x)$ changes its sign (from negative to positive or vice versa) at that point.\n\nWe summarize the information from the given graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-3,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,3)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $↗$ |  | $↘$ |  | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |\n| ${f}^{″}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $+$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $+$ |\n\nFrom the table, we conclude that ${f}^{″}(x)$ changes its sign at the points $x=-1$ and $x=0$ (note that ${f}^{″}(x)$ does not change the sign when it passes through $x=1$). Therefore, $x=-1$ and $x=0$ are  the points of inflection of $y=f(x).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516826 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175900 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cda8b343-e71d-5ec2-ae82-3f66dba09264" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Given the Graph of a Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "831d41b8-bd9e-5681-8c78-22285a833ee2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516391} {:answer/id #uuid "a8bb37db-fe44-5eec-a16e-9ca0e8df8a9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516464} {:answer/id #uuid "aa1d5f2d-417e-586f-9ace-9804af68af61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516468} {:answer/id #uuid "b62c0101-16f2-5788-97d4-6a5710cf73f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516493} {:answer/id #uuid "dc5ae25e-6d73-551e-a90f-5960d39695c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8bb37db-fe44-5eec-a16e-9ca0e8df8a9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516464} :answer-field/id #uuid "13ddd976-7c9f-580f-a147-d2bc02dfe62e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "90895ef1-1562-5ad7-aeaa-a3c532c8d41b" :question/math-academy-id "q-104028" :question/problem "![](images/19/19ebd6ce74bf6b25b920869403effa677c6a4bef2dedef38a81f77794c99a195.png)\n\nThe graph $y={h}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=h(x)$, is shown above. Which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\nI. $h(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-3$\n\nII. $h(x)$ has an inflection point on the interval $(0,2)$\n\nIII. $h(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements separately.\n\n- Statement I is false. The graph of $y={h}^{′}(x)$ does not change sign around $x=-3$, therefore, there cannot be a relative extremum at this point.\n\n- Statement II is true. The slope of the tangent line to $y={h}^{′}(x)$ changes its sign somewhere near $x=1.$ Hence, the graph $y=h(x)$ has an inflection point inside $(0,2).$\n\n- Statement III is false. The graph of $y={h}^{′}(x)$ does not change sign around $x=2$, therefore, there cannot be a relative extremum at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516807 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175900 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cda8b343-e71d-5ec2-ae82-3f66dba09264" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Given the Graph of a Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24002a8c-4053-5201-bc0c-28685f54124a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516180} {:answer/id #uuid "7458719f-c08d-5909-b6c4-2332f4752741" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516371} {:answer/id #uuid "a888ff1a-d08a-5b98-a570-ea8ced7b3307" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516462} {:answer/id #uuid "b96497cf-5c5b-55ec-980b-febea838617b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516499} {:answer/id #uuid "ba8a1bb5-c6cc-5faa-acf3-5cc85aaa3b08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24002a8c-4053-5201-bc0c-28685f54124a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516180} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbc98646-cfbc-5ee7-82cb-9e03b52e88b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "613b77ce-0261-57ef-a0c0-d6ed0660c89d" :question/math-academy-id "q-104037" :question/problem "![](images/6d/6d9d6bbd1f54319a6bf1aa8dae03d6ea7dc19e8430e7a5bfd72e805edbcfb6d5.png)\n\nThe graph $y={g}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=g(x)$, is shown above. Which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\nI. The graph of $y=g(x)$ is concave down on the interval $(-1,1)$\n\nII. $g(x)$ has a critical point at $x=-2$\n\nIII. $g(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements separately.\n\n- Statement I is false. The graph ${g}^{′}(x)$ is increasing on the interval $(0,1).$ Hence, ${g}^{″}(x)$ must be positive on $(0,1)$ and therefore, the graph of $y=g(x)$ is concave up on $(0,1)⊂(-1,1).$\n\n- Statement II is false. From the graph, we can conclude that ${g}^{′}(-2)$ exists and that it is not equal to $0.$ So, $x=-2$ is not a critical point of the function.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the first derivative ${g}^{′}(x)$ changes its sign at the point $x=1,$ from negative to positive. Therefore $g(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516841 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175900 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cda8b343-e71d-5ec2-ae82-3f66dba09264" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Given the Graph of a Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09f70fff-74e5-5199-bd4b-3d36c38c70d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ has a relative minimum and two inflection points" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516129} {:answer/id #uuid "41cffa6e-d5cc-5d5d-a843-ac6331011ca9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ has two relative minimum and one inflection point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516242} {:answer/id #uuid "58385d01-708f-525a-a0fd-c0e22b81dd95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ has two relative maximum and one inflection point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516298} {:answer/id #uuid "7d0bf746-9111-5462-8ba6-af6603c4c595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ has two relative extrema and three inflection points" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516377} {:answer/id #uuid "fe5ac39a-198c-5dfd-ad39-72d3c6e2c864" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ has a relative maximum and two inflection points" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d0bf746-9111-5462-8ba6-af6603c4c595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ has two relative extrema and three inflection points" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516377} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9cdc9c3-e9e6-55b0-85d0-226a22912581" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b66a1e23-ff78-5796-afef-31e3ad188600" :question/math-academy-id "q-104046" :question/problem "![](images/e5/e54c40efd2e67f41ab726eaf3ef13c56aab8acb56222e8db3c53de47e6bcf547.png)\n\nThe graph $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is shown above. Of the following, which best describes the function $y=f(x)$ on the interval $[-4,5]?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $y={f}^{′}(x)$ intersects the $x$-axis (where we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0$) twice, at the points $A,$ $B$ and $C$ shown below.\n\n![](images/76/76c04c26b2641990e4528fb3e89c4ef72c7a27c67e289c88cb1d6034baf67ef7.png)\n\nAn extremum of $y=f(x)$ occurs when the graph $y={f}^{′}(x)$ changes its sign. Hence, we have a relative minimum at $A,$  since the derivative goes from negative to positive, and a relative maximum at $B,$  since the derivative goes from positive to negative.\n\nNow, an inflection point may occur where the second derivative $y={f}^{″}(x)$ is zero (the tangent line to $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is horizontal) or $y={f}^{″}(x)$ does not exist (a rapid change in the slope). These are the points $C$, $D,$ $E,$ and $F$ as shown on the graph below.\n\n![](images/12/1264de8104784e808891fbe44b176e0624e05fa1ea8058cf533c732016eca63d.png)\n\n \n\nWe obtain an inflection point if the slope of the tangent line to $y={f}^{′}(x)$ changes its sign. Thus, we have inflection points only at $C,D,$ and $E.$ Indeed, at points $C$ and $E$ the slope goes from positive to negative, while at $D$ the slope goes from negative to positive.\n\nTherefore, we get two relative extrema and three inflection points on $[-2,5]$ for $y=f(x).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516776 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175947 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f73c50f7-fa8c-5d23-8699-e7e074f4251b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Inflection Points and Intervals of Concavity Given the Graph of a Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "393c5acb-26e9-5ef0-ad19-710d1bfb42a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "393c5acb-26e9-5ef0-ad19-710d1bfb42a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516230} :answer-field/id #uuid "26b788ac-a0f8-59c2-8a01-0ec009831d0f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516663} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a531457-4411-5f7f-9349-fe057e4a59ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a531457-4411-5f7f-9349-fe057e4a59ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516263} :answer-field/id #uuid "75ffc624-fd9a-513f-a70d-71e23491db92" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516707} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba2f3daf-be8b-5b31-8c93-24d592d01dba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba2f3daf-be8b-5b31-8c93-24d592d01dba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516502} :answer-field/id #uuid "e99ddf10-2ad1-5e8d-b98e-05b3db34966c" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516756}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "172429bc-7db9-5917-9fbf-cfae2b8af5b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-342885" :question/problem "![](images/15/1505bc3714405515ec7d5a834c6d08f849b3adbf8678a76b3b5cf27ccf394e28.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is defined on the interval $[-3,3]$ as shown above. In **ascending order**, the points of inflection of  $y=f(x)$ for $x\\in (-3,3)$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}, $\\,x=$\n\n{{field-2}}, $\\,x=$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "First, note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists at every point on the interval $x\\in [-3,3].$\n\nWe need to investigate the behavior of ${f}^{″}(x).$ We recall the following:\n\n- When ${f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{″}(x)>0.$\n\n- When ${f}^{′}(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ has an inflection point at $x=a,$ if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n- ${f}^{″}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{″}(x)$ is undefined, and\n\n- ${f}^{″}$ changes its sign (from negative to positive or vice versa) at that point\n\nWe summarize the information from the given graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-3,-2)$ | $-2$ | $(-2,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,3)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $↗$ |  | $↘$ |  | $↘$ |  | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |  | $↘$ |\n| ${f}^{″}$ | $+$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $-$ |\n\nFrom the table, we see that ${f}^{″}(x)$ changes its sign at the points $x=-2,$ $x=0,$ and $x=2$ (note that ${f}^{″}(x)$ does not change the sign when it passes through $x=-1$ and $x=1$).\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the points of inflection are \n\n$$\nx=-2,\\,x=0,\\,x=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516808 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175900 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cda8b343-e71d-5ec2-ae82-3f66dba09264" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Given the Graph of a Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1506571b-f473-5928-83bd-11a20fc0ef9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516152} {:answer/id #uuid "2707dfe2-84ea-5aa5-8685-b049f75c1c45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516191} {:answer/id #uuid "a46c0b10-4731-5f98-bc15-bdb6fcf71a3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516453} {:answer/id #uuid "c7b2d1e5-35ac-5046-99e3-a3b600bbe480" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516535} {:answer/id #uuid "f47e3b37-7280-5e88-99ac-68de6ea9db99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516620}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a46c0b10-4731-5f98-bc15-bdb6fcf71a3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516453} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d770aae-42c0-5f0b-871d-eb4f44a03763" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "627aa48c-c5a6-5e2c-bbad-89df3a735c3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-48933" :question/problem "![](images/30/30e2b42fd5b3ab5fb1ee538079b518fa4b98a637e81d7f9aad23b4dd718a4541.png)\n\nThe graph $y={h}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=h(x)$, is shown above. Which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\nI. $h(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-2$\n\nII. The graph of $y=h(x)$ is concave down on the interval $(-2,1)$\n\nIII. $h(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements separately.\n\n- Statement I is true. The graph $y={h}^{′}(x)$ intersects the $x$-axis at $x=-2$ meaning that ${h}^{′}(x)=0.$ Moreover, ${h}^{′}(x)$ changes its sign by going from positive to negative. Thus, there is a relative maximum at this point.\n\n- Statement II is false. The slope of the tangent line to $y={h}^{′}(x)$ changes its sign somewhere near $x=0.$ Hence, the graph $y=h(x)$ has an inflection point inside $(-2,1).$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have that ${h}^{′}(3)=0$ but ${h}^{′}(x)$ does not change its sign around this point. Thus, there is no extremum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516781 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175900 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cda8b343-e71d-5ec2-ae82-3f66dba09264" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Given the Graph of a Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13c651e1-b750-5b3b-853a-335582693525" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g(x)$ has one relative maximum, one relative minimum, and one inflection point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516150} {:answer/id #uuid "1af5da52-60e7-52ce-a067-eb10b1254363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g(x)$ has one relative maximum, one relative minimum, and two inflection points" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516163} {:answer/id #uuid "1bf67587-3e1f-5acf-9d8f-828394e302f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g(x)$ has one relative minimum and one inflection point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516165} {:answer/id #uuid "274f6761-6dee-5f58-970a-634abfeec5f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g(x)$ has one relative maximum and two inflection points" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516193} {:answer/id #uuid "81d0c727-e883-5ab7-9182-e230e6ef6478" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g(x)$ has two relative minima and two inflection points" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516386}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "274f6761-6dee-5f58-970a-634abfeec5f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g(x)$ has one relative maximum and two inflection points" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516193} :answer-field/id #uuid "6626c773-e0b2-5ef5-874e-85aeb86e599a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516695}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1cc6f806-f012-5062-b71f-ffac34d675d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-49086" :question/problem "![](images/5a/5a753c70df3b523ccdeead554bee653ebba355d41ef95aea7ace17236fd38829.png)\n\nThe graph $y={g}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=g(x)$, is shown above. Of the following, which best describes the function $y=g(x)$ on the interval $[-4,4]?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $y={g}^{′}(x)$ intersects the $x$-axis (where we have ${g}^{′}(x)=0$) twice, at the points $A$ and $B$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/42/42e073c044b6bf9ced663bcb55bbd89d7ac79f85f918ca3364d2be3b2f8e74bc.png)\n\nAn extremum of $y=g(x)$ occurs when the graph $y={g}^{′}(x)$ changes its sign. Hence, we have an extremum at $B$ only. This is a relative maximum since the derivative goes from positive to negative.\n\nNow, an inflection point may occur where the second derivative $y={g}^{″}(x)$ is zero (the tangent line to $y={g}^{′}(x)$ is horizontal) or $y={g}^{″}(x)$ does not exist (a rapid change in the slope). These are points the $A$, $C$ and $D$ on the graph.\n\n![](images/65/650e68ff0dfbb6f0b679559a46532f2e29fb970f392a6bd4932b8363c2aa6777.png)\n\nWe obtain an inflection point if the slope of the tangent line to $y={g}^{′}(x)$ changes its sign. Thus, we have an inflection point at $A$, where the slope goes from negative to positive, and an inflection point at $C$, where the slope goes from positive to negative.\n\nTherefore, we get one relative maximum and two inflection points on $[-4,4]$ for $y=g(x).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176574 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175947 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f73c50f7-fa8c-5d23-8699-e7e074f4251b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Inflection Points and Intervals of Concavity Given the Graph of a Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0139d7bf-fb60-5bb3-895a-29c8b2baffc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=\\pm 1$ and $x=\\pm 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516114} {:answer/id #uuid "8f6cec58-1be3-5172-8721-f21acb7fd27f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516412} {:answer/id #uuid "cebddd16-be70-5c64-b343-e678dc075919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516551} {:answer/id #uuid "d1df2cbc-6331-51a2-b674-f40ca0e480f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516556} {:answer/id #uuid "e9e68976-17e1-5b7d-962e-22b6597e86d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=\\pm 1$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f6cec58-1be3-5172-8721-f21acb7fd27f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516412} :answer-field/id #uuid "b944462a-0d9c-56d1-ba28-a212c5ca0888" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2b96e246-6e1a-5cb7-9888-3f16ec267c72" :question/math-academy-id "q-80032" :question/problem "![](images/19/199a0809c86f57743fe954d1221656198f92339f146722928b46e598680d299f.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is defined on the interval $[-3,3]$ as shown above. Find all of the inflection points of $y=f(x)$ for $x\\in (-3,3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists at every point on the interval $x\\in [-3,3].$\n\nWe need to investigate the behavior of ${f}^{″}(x).$ We recall the following:\n\n- When ${f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{″}(x)>0.$\n\n- When ${f}^{′}(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ has an inflection point at $x=a,$ if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n- ${f}^{″}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{″}(x)$ is undefined, and\n\n- ${f}^{″}(x)$ changes its sign (from negative to positive or vice versa) at that point.\n\nWe summarize the information from the given graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-3,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,3)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $↗$ |  | $↘$ |  | $↗$ |\n| ${f}^{″}$ | $+$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $-$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $+$ |\n\nFrom the table, we conclude that ${f}^{″}(x)$ changes its sign at the points $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ Therefore, $x=-1$ and $x=1$ are the points of inflection of $y=f(x).$"}]}
